\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 License requirements\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Do not assume that an open-source repository automatically permits commercial distribution of its pretrained weights.\
Verify the licenses of code, checkpoints, dependencies, and any bundled assets individually. Document the original authors, publications, license notices, and attribution requirements.\
Do not download or redistribute weights unless their provenance and usage rights have been checked.\
If a candidate is incompatible with the intended distribution model, select a compatible alternative or explain the blocker.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Recommended implementation strategy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use a staged approach:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Stage A \'97 Working baseline:
\f1\b0  Implement a transparent, well-documented signal-processing baseline, such as POS or CHROM, if its assumptions and implementation are appropriate for the captured video. This makes it possible to test the camera-to-signal pipeline before neural inference is integrated.\

\f0\b Stage B \'97 Neural model:
\f1\b0  Add the best suitable pretrained model behind the same inference interface if a compatible, licensed model and supported runtime are available.\

\f0\b Stage C \'97 Evaluation:
\f1\b0  Compare the baseline and neural model using reproducible offline evaluation, signal-quality metrics, latency measurements, and documented test data.\
Do not pretend that a baseline algorithm is a trained neural network. Clearly identify which algorithm is active in the UI and documentation.\
If model weights are unavailable or conversion cannot be completed, keep the application functional where possible, expose an explicit unavailable-model state, and document the remaining integration work. Do not fabricate inference results.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

